---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
// The release the front page names; change it with each release (docs/RELEASING.md in the app repo).
const version = '0.1.4';
const snippet = `Every build and test goes through LoadTable:
  loadtable run --who YOUR_AGENT --anywhere -- <command>
--anywhere picks a free machine, --peer NAME picks one,
--pin keeps the job on this machine (GUIs, dev servers).
Sessions with MCP can use the loadtable tools instead.
Check \`loadtable list --active\` before starting. If a call
times out, query the printed job id; never resubmit.
Name test targets: cargo test -p X --lib FILTER.`;
---
<Layout title="LoadTable — one build queue for all your coding agents">
  <main id="main" class="wrap">
    <div class="hero">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <span class="label">Five agents. One laptop. Nobody freezes.</span>
        <h1>A build queue for your coding agents.</h1>
        <p class="lede">Claude Code, Codex, Cursor and the rest all reach for <code>cargo test</code> and <code>gradlew</code> at the same moment. <b>LoadTable</b> queues their heavy commands, runs each one on whichever of your machines can take it, and keeps a record of who ran what, where, and how long it took.</p>
        <div class="install" aria-label="Install command"><code><span class="p">$ </span><span id="install-cmd">curl -fsSL https://loadtable.osvauld.com/install.sh | sh</span></code><button class="btn" type="button" data-copy="install-cmd">Copy</button></div>
        <p class="release"><span>v{version}</span><span>Linux and macOS</span><span>MIT</span><a href="/docs/install">Install guide</a><a href="/docs/status">Status</a></p>
      </div>

      <figure class="shot">
        <a class="when-light" href="/shots/window-light.webp"><img src="/shots/hero-light.webp" width="760" height="700" alt="The LoadTable window: three machines with their memory, CPU and slots, then four running jobs and four waiting ones, each with its owner, machine and reason for waiting." fetchpriority="high"></a>
        <a class="when-dark" href="/shots/window-dark.webp"><img src="/shots/hero-dark.webp" width="760" height="700" alt="The LoadTable window: three machines with their memory, CPU and slots, then four running jobs and four waiting ones, each with its owner, machine and reason for waiting." fetchpriority="high"></a>
        <figcaption>The desktop window, <code>loadtable ui</code>, with example data. <a class="when-light" href="/shots/window-light.webp">See it with a job open</a><a class="when-dark" href="/shots/window-dark.webp">See it with a job open</a></figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <section class="band" id="run">
      <div class="band-head"><span class="label">A real run</span><h2>One command, the right machine</h2><p>An agent on the laptop runs its tests; <code>--anywhere</code> sends them to the Linux desktop next to it. Output trimmed and paths shortened; nothing else changed.</p></div>
      <div class="band-body">
<pre class="term"><span class="p">$ </span>loadtable run --who claude-a1 --anywhere -- cargo test -p tasks-core --lib -j 6
loadtable: submitted job 1721; … Ctrl-C detaches, loadtable cancel 1721 cancels
<span class="lt">[loadtable] placement:</span> nixos (sent here by a peer, which chose this machine; the job runs where it was sent)
<span class="lt">[loadtable] cache:</span> sccache ~/build-cache/sccache, target -&gt; ~/build-cache/…/target
<span class="lt">[loadtable]</span> cargo -j 6 lowered to 3 (machine busy: cap 3)
<span class="lt">[loadtable] confine:</span> this job came from another machine, so it runs confined: it cannot open anything in ~, /root, /run/media except: read and write ~/build-cache, ~/.cargo, ~/.rustup, …
    Finished `test` profile [unoptimized] target(s) in 11.28s
running 50 tests
<span class="dim">…</span>
test result: ok. 50 passed; 0 failed; 0 ignored; 0 measured; 0 filtered out; finished in 0.01s

<span class="ok">job 1721 passed (exit 0) owner=claude-a1 on nixos</span></pre>
        <ul class="notes">
          <li><h3>Placed where it can start</h3><p><code>--anywhere</code> picks the machine that can start soonest. <code>--peer NAME</code> names one; <code>--pin</code> keeps it here.</p></li>
          <li><h3>The checkout travels, not the repo</h3><p>Only the commits and edits the worker lacks are sent, over an encrypted link between paired machines.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Warm caches, sane parallelism</h3><p>sccache and <code>target/</code> live on the worker's cache disk, and <code>-j</code> is cut to what the machine can take right now.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Results come home</h3><p>The log streams back live and <code>run</code> exits with the job's exit code. <code>--fetch</code> brings files such as an APK back into the checkout.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="band-head"><span class="label">Why</span><h2>Every agent thinks it has the machine to itself</h2></div>
      <div class="band-body">
        <ul class="pa">
          <li><div><h3>Everything starts at once</h3><p>Five sessions each start a full Rust test build. Memory fills, swap thrashes, the desktop stops responding.</p></div>
            <div class="ans"><p>Each machine admits work by real memory, CPU and I/O pressure and by slots, so a job starts when it can finish. The rest wait their turn.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>The disk fills up</h3><p>Every agent's worktree grows its own <code>target/</code>, often tens of GB. Five agents fill a laptop disk, and then everything fails.</p></div>
            <div class="ans"><p>One sccache per machine, each checkout's <code>target/</code> on a cache disk you choose, and a disk reserve that holds jobs back before the disk is full. <code>loadtable gc</code> frees the output of merged and deleted worktrees on every machine.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>The right machine sits idle</h3><p>The desktop has spare cores and Apple builds need the Mac on the shelf, but getting work there means SSH scripts and copied folders.</p></div>
            <div class="ans"><p>Pair the machines once. Jobs go to whichever can start soonest; Apple builds go to the Mac, phone jobs to the machine with the phone.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>Nobody knows when it will finish</h3><p>An agent's tool call gives up after ten minutes and starts the same build again, on top of the first.</p></div>
            <div class="ans"><p>Every queued and running job has an estimate from past runs of the same command, warm or cold, as a range from the fastest recent run to the slowest. Agents are told to wait on job N, not resubmit.</p></div></li>
          <li><div><h3>Nobody knows what ran</h3><p>Which agent started that 45-minute build? Did it pass? The answer is scattered across terminal scrollback.</p></div>
            <div class="ans"><p>Who, what, where, how long, exit code and the full log, kept in a ledger and a signed audit log: <code>loadtable logs</code>, <code>log-search</code>, or the window.</p></div></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="pairing">
      <div class="band-head"><span class="label">Pairing</span><h2>Add a machine in two commands</h2><p>Install LoadTable on each machine, then pair them. No SSH keys, no open ports.</p></div>
      <div class="band-body">
<pre class="term"><span class="dim"># on the machine that will take work: prints a one-use ticket, valid 15 minutes</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>loadtable pair invite --both-ways

<span class="dim"># on your laptop</span>
<span class="p">$ </span>loadtable pair accept &lt;ticket&gt;
<span class="p">$ </span>loadtable peers</pre>
        <ul class="notes">
          <li><h3>Reaches through any network</h3><p>On the same network machines connect directly; elsewhere through a relay that forwards encrypted traffic and cannot read it.</p></li>
          <li><h3>You choose what each may do</h3><p><code>submit</code> sends jobs, <code>observe</code> watches, <code>admin</code> drains and resumes. <code>--both-ways</code> grants the same roles back.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Revoke at any time</h3><p><code>loadtable peers revoke NAME</code> drops its grants, closes its connections and stops sending it work.</p></li>
          <li><h3>Name your machines</h3><p>Placement uses machine names, so <code>--peer desktop</code> and <code>--peer mac</code> mean what you expect.</p></li>
        </ul>
        <p><a href="/docs/pairing">Pairing in detail →</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="setup">
      <div class="band-head"><span class="label">Toolchains</span><h2>Each machine brings its own toolchains</h2><p>LoadTable runs your commands; it does not install compilers. A worker needs LoadTable and whatever its jobs use.</p></div>
      <div class="band-body">
        <div class="fit">
          <div class="yes"><h3>Send it as a job</h3>
            <ul>
              <li><code>loadtable run --peer mac -- rustup target add aarch64-apple-ios</code></li>
              <li><code>rustup toolchain install</code> and <code>cargo install</code>, into <code>~/.cargo</code> and <code>~/.rustup</code></li>
              <li>Android SDK packages with <code>sdkmanager</code>; Gradle fetches its own dependencies</li>
              <li>Recorded like every other job</li>
            </ul></div>
          <div class="no"><h3>Do it once on the machine</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Anything that needs <code>sudo</code>: <code>apt</code>, <code>dnf</code>, <code>pacman</code></li>
              <li>Homebrew, which writes outside the folders a job may reach</li>
              <li>Xcode and its command line tools</li>
              <li>LoadTable itself and its service, <code>loadtabled install</code></li>
            </ul></div>
        </div>
        <p>A job from another machine may write only the build cache, its checkout, temp and the user toolchains (<code>~/.cargo</code>, <code>~/.rustup</code>, <code>~/.gradle</code>, the Android SDK, <code>~/Library/Developer</code>), and it cannot use <code>sudo</code>. That is the line between the two lists.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="safety">
      <div class="band-head"><span class="label">Safety</span><h2>A paired machine runs commands on yours</h2><p>So LoadTable bounds what those commands can reach, and writes down every one of them.</p></div>
      <div class="band-body">
        <div class="feats">
          <div class="feat"><h3>Confined by the OS</h3><p>Jobs from other machines run under Landlock (Linux) or Seatbelt (macOS): the cache, their checkout and the toolchains, nothing else in your home folder. A job that cannot be confined is refused.</p></div>
          <div class="feat"><h3>Roles per machine</h3><p>Each pairing is a signed grant bound to the other machine's key, with its roles and an optional expiry. Every request is checked against it.</p></div>
          <div class="feat"><h3>Encrypted link</h3><p>Machines talk over an encrypted peer-to-peer link. A relay can see that two machines talk, not what they say; turn it off for direct links only.</p></div>
          <div class="feat"><h3>Secrets stay home</h3><p>Files that look like credentials (<code>.env</code>, SSH keys, <code>*.pem</code>, keystores) are not synced unless the repository tracks them.</p></div>
          <div class="feat"><h3>A record that shows edits</h3><p>Every job, refusal, pairing and grant change is in the audit log, with checkpoints signed by the machine's key. <code>loadtable audit</code> reads it.</p></div>
          <div class="feat"><h3>Personal devices off limits</h3><p>Deny a phone's serial in the machine policy and every job that names it is refused, also when another machine sends it.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="fit">
          <div class="no"><h3>What it does not do</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>Jobs you submit on a machine yourself run as you, unconfined</li>
              <li>A job has the network, and can send what it can read</li>
              <li>A job that changes a toolchain changes it for your own builds too</li>
              <li>So pair only machines you would give an SSH login</li>
            </ul></div>
        </div>
        <p><a href="/docs/security">The security model →</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="start">
      <div class="band-head"><span class="label">Tell your agents</span><h2>One paragraph in AGENTS.md</h2><p>Paste this into the repo's AGENTS.md or CLAUDE.md. Sessions with MCP get the same through the tools.</p></div>
      <div class="band-body">
        <div class="snippet">
          <button class="btn ghost" type="button" data-copy="snip">Copy</button>
          <pre id="snip">{snippet}</pre>
        </div>
        <p><a href="/docs/agents">Setting up agents →</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="band-head"><span class="label">Who it's for</span><h2>Small teams with real hardware</h2></div>
      <div class="band-body">
        <div class="fit">
          <div class="yes"><h3>A good fit if you</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>run two or more coding agents at the same time</li>
              <li>build heavy things: Rust workspaces, Android with Gradle, iOS and macOS apps</li>
              <li>have a second machine: an old laptop, a desktop, a Mac mini</li>
              <li>want to know afterwards what ran, where, and whether it passed</li>
            </ul></div>
          <div class="no"><h3>Not what you need if you</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>run one agent on projects that build in seconds</li>
              <li>already send every build to cloud CI or a build farm</li>
              <li>want to split one compile across machines, like distcc or Bazel remote execution</li>
              <li>run a fleet of servers; this is for a handful of personal machines</li>
            </ul></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band" id="built-on">
      <div class="band-head"><span class="label">Built on</span><h2>Written in Rust, on open source</h2><p>LoadTable is MIT licensed. These projects do the heavy lifting.</p></div>
      <div class="band-body">
        <ul class="notes">
          <li><h3><a href="https://iroh.computer">iroh</a></h3><p>The encrypted peer-to-peer links between machines, direct or relayed, and the file transfers over them.</p></li>
          <li><h3><a href="https://github.com/GitoxideLabs/gitoxide">gitoxide</a></h3><p>Reads the checkout in pure Rust, so only the commits and edits a worker lacks are sent.</p></li>
          <li><h3><a href="https://www.egui.rs">egui</a></h3><p>The desktop window, <code>loadtable ui</code>, drawn with egui and eframe.</p></li>
          <li><h3><a href="https://www.sqlite.org">SQLite</a></h3><p>The job ledger and the audit log on each machine.</p></li>
          <li><h3><a href="https://landlock.io">Landlock</a> and Seatbelt</h3><p>The operating system sandboxes that confine jobs from other machines, on Linux and macOS.</p></li>
          <li><h3><a href="https://github.com/mozilla/sccache">sccache</a></h3><p>The compile cache every cargo build on a machine shares.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="band-head"><span class="label">Compared</span><h2>Next to the tools you have</h2><p>These move whole sessions or single compile steps. LoadTable schedules the heavy commands underneath them, so it works alongside all of them.</p></div>
      <div class="band-body">
        <div class="tbl">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What moves</th><th>Shares resources between agents</th><th>Multiple machines</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Remote agent sessions</td><td>The whole agent session</td><td>No, each session builds on its own</td><td>Yes, one session per seat</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Vendor cloud agents</td><td>The whole session, to vendor machines</td><td>No</td><td>Vendor machines only</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Single-machine command queues</td><td>Nothing; commands wait their turn</td><td>Yes, on one machine</td><td>No</td></tr>
              <tr><td>distcc, sccache-dist, Bazel RE</td><td>Single compile steps</td><td>Not agent-aware</td><td>Yes, with build-system setup</td></tr>
              <tr class="us"><td>LoadTable</td><td>Heavy commands: builds, tests, device runs</td><td>Yes, by pressure, slots and queue class</td><td>Yes, your own paired machines</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="band">
      <div class="band-head"><span class="label">Where it stands</span><h2>Early, and used every day</h2><p>Shaped by written reports from the agents that use it on real projects.</p></div>
      <div class="band-body">
        <div class="status">
          <div><span class="pill ok">Works today</span><ul>
            <li>Linux laptop with Linux and macOS workers</li>
            <li>Queue, admission, queue classes, estimates</li>
            <li>Peer sync, log streaming, fetching outputs</li>
            <li>Build caches, MCP server, desktop window</li>
            <li>Freeing build output on every machine with <code>gc</code></li>
            <li>Each machine's exact build in <code>status</code></li></ul></div>
          <div><span class="pill run">In progress</span><ul>
            <li>Confinement on macOS workers, still new</li>
            <li>Suggesting split steps for Gradle build-and-test</li></ul></div>
          <div><span class="pill wait">Not yet</span><ul>
            <li>Moving a waiting job when another machine frees up</li>
            <li>A Mac worker that goes to sleep</li>
            <li>Emptying idle caches automatically</li>
            <li>GPU and file requirements for placement</li></ul></div>
        </div>
        <p><a href="/docs/status">Full status and known gaps →</a></p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <script src="../scripts/home.js"></script>
</Layout>
